  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Introduction of indoor crop cultivation among small-scale farmers</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">25 participants Train-the-Trainer (ToT) training course</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Regional Farmers Development Association (RFDA) &#x2013; Yangon has been established in November 2017. The Association represented farmers in Kayan and Thongwa townships and they welcomed new farmers members from other townships in Yangon Region. Under P4 (Pulses, People, Planet and Profit), funded by UKAID and jointly implemented by NAG (Network Activity Group), EWS (East West Seed) and ICCO Myanmar, Yangon RFDA received several capacity building trainings for Agriculture, Organization Development and Leadership. On 26 August 2019, RFDA started the registration process and they received Registration on 9 October 2020. The registration is valid for five years. RFDA is not connected with Government departments in 2021. Cordaid will facilitate a 20 days training of trainers of the RFDA. RFDA has a long term plan to build the capacity of farmers. RFDA provides training to farmers in different topics and have confidence to give trainings such as GAP, Leadership, Soil Preparation, Covid 19 Awareness and Performance Review. New technologies for crops and vegetables are most expected. Currently at least 5 farmer leaders in RFDA are trainers to other farmers. However Yangon RFDA wants to build a well-trained pool of experienced lead farmers and extension staff, ready to take on board and induce systematically new lead-farmers (especially women) to reach out in a pyramid system to at least 15% of the members in Kayan and Thone-gwa Townships in one year and realize with them the required increase sustainable crops</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The training set-up is based on 2 main objectives:

1. Participants will acquire relevant knowledge and skills to improve their farming practices and farm income

2. Participants will be able to share these with (other) farmers in their cooperative and wider community.</narrative>
  </description>
